Summary: krnl386: when LDT entries are refused, load without 16-bit support and fail 16-bit calls cleanly
Category: system
Kind: fix
Origin: wine-mac-builds
  Version: 11.22
  Changes: ldt_entries_supported passes 10.0's LDT_ENTRY arguments
    (null_entry) to its four-argument NtSetLdtEntries
Upstream: no
Why: kernel32's 16-bit thunks (LoadLibrary16, GetProcAddress16,
 FreeLibrary16 and the like) are forwarded to krnl386.exe16, so a 32-bit
 DLL that imports any of them makes the loader load krnl386.exe16 and run
 its attach, even if it never calls one. The attach loads the 16-bit
 KERNEL, which needs LDT entries for its segments. Under Rosetta 2,
 NtSetLdtEntries refuses them (ntdll-rosetta-refuse-ldt-entries), krnl386 ignored that, and the load
 faulted on a segment that was never set up (Wine 11.22: a write to address
 0xc22). The attach failed, and with it the importing DLL. Now the attach
 first checks that LDT entries can be set, and if not, succeeds without
 loading the 16-bit KERNEL or giving threads a 16-bit stack. A 16-bit call
 made anyway then fails with an error in the log instead of a fault:
 loading a 16-bit module (LoadLibrary16, LoadModule16, and ThunkConnect32,
 which loads the 16-bit side of a thunk) returns 2, file not found, and a
 call into 16-bit code (WOWCallback16Ex, which the 32-to-16 thunks go
 through) returns FALSE. Before, both faulted in the same way as the
 attach. Where LDT entries work nothing changes.
 On 10.0 nothing needs to come before it: its x86_64 NtSetLdtEntries
 always returns STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED, as ntdll-rosetta-refuse-ldt-entries
 makes it under Rosetta 2 on 11.x.
Programs: Webmaster (Tivola, a Director 7 projector): Director reports
 "Handler not defined #baRegister", as its Buddy API Xtra (Budapi.x32),
 which imports LoadLibrary16, is unloaded straight after it maps.
 Bioscopia ships a budapi32.dll beside its Budapi Xtra that imports
 LoadLibrary16, FreeLibrary16 and GetProcAddress16 (found by
 tools/scan16; not run)
Uncertain: what a program does when a 16-bit call fails; Budapi's
 functions that Webmaster uses make none. A 32-to-16 thunk can only be
 called after ThunkConnect32 succeeds, which it now can't, so the thunks'
 own code past WOWCallback16Ex (which reads the 16-bit stack that isn't
 there) shouldn't run.
Status: 2026-11-18: ported from 11.22. 2026-11-19: CI run 44332082425
 failed to compile it: 10.0's NtSetLdtEntries takes two LDT_ENTRY values
 (four arguments), not 11.x's six ULONGs. ldt_entries_supported now passes
 null_entry; the patched krnl386 sources pass a syntax check with
 llvm-mingw (which does catch the old call). Not yet in a build.
 krnl386-attach fails on macfixes-10.0-build7 (krnl386.exe16 doesn't load, error
 998), as on 11.22 before this patch.

diff --git a/dlls/krnl386.exe16/kernel.c b/dlls/krnl386.exe16/kernel.c
--- a/dlls/krnl386.exe16/kernel.c
+++ b/dlls/krnl386.exe16/kernel.c
@@ -39,6 +39,11 @@
 
 static DWORD process_dword;
 
+/* Set when the process can't run 16-bit code (no LDT entries, as under
+   Rosetta 2): the 16-bit KERNEL isn't loaded and threads get no 16-bit
+   stack. */
+BOOL no_16bit;
+
 /***********************************************************************
  *           KERNEL thread initialisation routine
  */
@@ -73,13 +78,23 @@
     {
     case DLL_PROCESS_ATTACH:
         init_selectors();
+        /* Without LDT entries 16-bit code can't run.  Still load: 32-bit
+           programs and DLLs that only import kernel32's 16-bit thunks
+           (LoadLibrary16 and the like, forwarded here) need this DLL to
+           load, and the 16-bit calls themselves then fail. */
+        if (!ldt_entries_supported())
+        {
+            WARN( "this process can't set LDT entries; 16-bit code won't run\n" );
+            no_16bit = TRUE;
+            break;
+        }
         if (LoadLibrary16( "krnl386.exe" ) < 32) return FALSE;
         /* fall through */
     case DLL_THREAD_ATTACH:
-        thread_attach();
+        if (!no_16bit) thread_attach();
         break;
     case DLL_THREAD_DETACH:
-        thread_detach();
+        if (!no_16bit) thread_detach();
         break;
     }
     return TRUE;
diff --git a/dlls/krnl386.exe16/kernel16_private.h b/dlls/krnl386.exe16/kernel16_private.h
--- a/dlls/krnl386.exe16/kernel16_private.h
+++ b/dlls/krnl386.exe16/kernel16_private.h
@@ -247,6 +247,8 @@
 static inline unsigned char ldt_get_flags( WORD sel ) { return ldt_copy->flags[sel >> 3]; }
 
 extern void init_selectors(void);
+extern BOOL ldt_entries_supported(void);
+extern BOOL no_16bit;
 extern BOOL ldt_is_system( WORD sel );
 extern BOOL ldt_is_valid( WORD sel );
 extern void *ldt_get_ptr( WORD sel, DWORD offset );
diff --git a/dlls/krnl386.exe16/ne_module.c b/dlls/krnl386.exe16/ne_module.c
--- a/dlls/krnl386.exe16/ne_module.c
+++ b/dlls/krnl386.exe16/ne_module.c
@@ -1112,6 +1112,12 @@
     if (name == NULL) return 0;
 
     TRACE("name %s, paramBlock %p\n", name, paramBlock);
+
+    if (no_16bit)
+    {
+        ERR("can't load 16-bit module %s: this process can't run 16-bit code\n", debugstr_a(name));
+        return ERROR_FILE_NOT_FOUND;
+    }
 
     /* Load module */
 
diff --git a/dlls/krnl386.exe16/selector.c b/dlls/krnl386.exe16/selector.c
--- a/dlls/krnl386.exe16/selector.c
+++ b/dlls/krnl386.exe16/selector.c
@@ -75,6 +75,19 @@
 }
 
 /***********************************************************************
+ *           ldt_entries_supported
+ *
+ * Whether this process can set LDT entries, which all 16-bit code needs.
+ * Under Rosetta 2 on macOS, NtSetLdtEntries refuses them
+ * (STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED).  Sets the first free entry to the null entry it
+ * already is.
+ */
+BOOL ldt_entries_supported(void)
+{
+    return !NtSetLdtEntries( (first_ldt_entry << 3) | 7, null_entry, 0, null_entry );
+}
+
+/***********************************************************************
  *           ldt_is_system
  */
 BOOL ldt_is_system( WORD sel )
diff --git a/dlls/krnl386.exe16/wowthunk.c b/dlls/krnl386.exe16/wowthunk.c
--- a/dlls/krnl386.exe16/wowthunk.c
+++ b/dlls/krnl386.exe16/wowthunk.c
@@ -432,8 +432,15 @@
      * (both for PASCAL and CDECL calling convention), so we simply
      * copy them to the 16-bit stack ...
      */
-    char *stack = (char *)CURRENT_STACK16 - cbArgs;
+    char *stack;
 
+    if (no_16bit)
+    {
+        ERR("can't call 16-bit code: this process can't run 16-bit code\n");
+        return FALSE;
+    }
+    stack = (char *)CURRENT_STACK16 - cbArgs;
+
     memcpy( stack, pArgs, cbArgs );
 
     if (dwFlags & WCB16_REGS)
